<!-- 01 -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The teacher-forced drafter</div>
<h2>How drafters are trained today — and the hidden mismatch</h2>
<p>A speculative-decoding drafter is a tiny model that proposes the next few tokens so the big <b>target</b> model can verify them in one parallel forward pass. Modern drafters (EAGLE, EAGLE-2, and the EAGLE-3 heads produced by frameworks like <b>SpecForge</b>) are not standalone language models — they consume the target's hidden states and autoregress a short chain of draft tokens on top of them.</p>
<p>The standard recipe is <b>offline and teacher-forced</b>: collect a corpus of responses, run the target once to dump its hidden states and tokens, then train the drafter to predict the next token/feature given the <i>target's true</i> context at every position. The drafter is, in effect, an attachment trained against a frozen tape of perfect inputs.</p>
<p>Here's the catch. At inference, after the first draft token, the drafter no longer sees the target's true context — it must condition on <i>its own</i> previously drafted tokens and features. The input distribution it was trained on and the one it actually runs on are <b>different</b>. Toggle the two modes below.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">drafter_context.viz — train vs. inference inputs</span><span class="vp-hint">toggle the mode</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="viz-controls">
      <button class="viz-btn active" id="ctx-train">Training (teacher-forced)</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="ctx-infer">Inference (free-running)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="ctx-canvas" height="250"></canvas>
    <div class="viz-caption" id="ctx-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>In training, every input cell is fed by the target's <b>green</b> ground-truth token. At inference, each cell is fed by the drafter's <b>own</b> previous output — and a single wrong guess ("a" instead of "the") drops the drafter onto a sequence it never saw in training, so every later position is now off-manifold. This is textbook <b>exposure bias</b>, and it's exactly what on-policy distillation was invented to fix.</p>
</section>

<!-- 02 -->
<section id="s-lossless" data-code="lossless">
<div class="section-no">02 — Acceptance is the only currency</div>
<h2>Why a drafter is unlike any other distilled student</h2>
<p>Before asking "would on-policy help," we have to be precise about what "help" means here — because speculative decoding is <b>lossless</b>. The target verifies each draft token and, on the first rejection, resamples from a corrected distribution. The final text is provably <i>identical</i> to what the target would have produced on its own. A bad drafter never corrupts the output; it only makes you verify more often.</p>
<p>So unlike normal knowledge distillation — where a worse student means worse answers — a worse drafter means only <b>slower</b> generation. The single metric that matters is the <b>acceptance length τ</b>: how many draft tokens survive verification per target forward pass. Drag the alignment slider and watch the output stay identical while speed changes.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">verify.viz — accept / reject / resample</span><span class="vp-hint">drag the slider</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="ver-canvas" height="170"></canvas>
    <div class="slider-row">
      <label>drafter&nbsp;alignment</label>
      <input type="range" id="ver-slider" min="1" max="6" step="1" value="2">
      <label id="ver-val">low</label>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-num" id="ver-tau">2</div><div class="stat-cap">accept length τ</div></div>
      <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-num" id="ver-tps">3.0×</div><div class="stat-cap">speedup vs. target</div></div>
      <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-num" style="color:var(--green)">identical</div><div class="stat-cap">output quality</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="viz-caption">Final text is <b>always</b> "Paris, the capital of France." — the verifier guarantees it. The drafter's quality only moves the <b>speed</b> dial. <b>That reframes the whole question</b>: distilling the drafter can never hurt accuracy, so the only risk of any training change is a lower acceptance rate.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>This is liberating. We can be as aggressive as we like with on-policy methods, exotic divergences, or self-generated data — there is no quality downside to fear, only an acceptance-rate (speed) payoff to chase.</p>
</section>

<!-- 03 -->
<section id="s-exposure" data-code="exposure">
<div class="section-no">03 — Exposure bias: εT² vs εT</div>
<h2>The imitation-learning law that predicts the win</h2>
<p>Why should training on self-generated drafts beat teacher forcing? The answer predates LLMs. Training a sequence model on clean expert prefixes is <b>behavior cloning</b>; running it on its own outputs is a sequential decision process. The classic result (Ross &amp; Bagnell's DAgger analysis) is that per-step error <span class="math-var">ε</span> compounds <b>quadratically</b> in horizon T under behavior cloning, but only <b>linearly</b> when the model is trained on the distribution of states it actually visits.</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">compounding error bound</div>
offline (teacher-forced):&nbsp;&nbsp;regret = O(<span class="math-var">ε</span> · T²)<br>
on-policy (DAgger-style):&nbsp;&nbsp;regret = O(<span class="math-var">ε</span> · T)<br>
<span class="math-comment"># ε = per-step drafter error,  T = draft window length</span>
</div>

<p>For a drafter the "horizon" is the draft window — usually short (T ≈ 5–8 tokens, or a small token tree). But the quadratic term still bites: the offline drafter's drift accumulates fastest exactly across the positions that decide acceptance length. The chart shows the gap.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">compounding.viz — drift vs. draft-window length</span><span class="vp-hint">hover the curves</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="exp-canvas" height="260"></canvas>
    <div class="viz-caption">The <b style="color:#ef6461">red</b> curve (offline / teacher-forced) is the drafter's expected drift off the target's manifold; it grows like <b>εT²</b>. The <b style="color:#34d399">green</b> curve (on-policy) grows like <b>εT</b>. Even at a modest window of T=6, the offline drafter has drifted far enough that the last drafted tokens are routinely rejected — capping acceptance length. On-policy training keeps the tail of the draft window on-manifold, which is precisely where the extra accepted tokens come from.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<p>DistillSpec makes this rigorous for drafters specifically: it proves (their Theorem 4.1) that a small <i>on-policy</i> distillation loss inductively bounds the per-token divergence at <i>every</i> position of the window — which is exactly the chain that determines acceptance.</p>
</section>

<!-- 04 -->
<section id="s-onpolicy" data-code="onpolicy">
<div class="section-no">04 — Feeding the drafter its own drafts</div>
<h2>The frontier already does this — it's called training-time test</h2>
<p>Here's the punchline: the user's instinct isn't speculative — it's the dominant direction of drafter research over the last two years. Both <b>HASS</b> ("harmonized context alignment") and <b>EAGLE-3</b> ("training-time test", the recipe SpecForge implements) train the drafter across multiple unrolled steps, replacing the target's clean feature with the drafter's <i>own</i> feature from the previous step. That <b>is</b> on-policy distillation of the drafter. Step through it:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">training_time_test.py — multi-step on-policy unroll</span><span class="vp-hint">use Next ▸</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="ttt-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
