<p>EAGLE-3 adds a second, related move: it <b>drops the feature-regression (MSE) loss</b> that older EAGLE used to force the drafter's hidden state to match the target's. That constraint, the authors found, "limits the expressiveness of the draft model and makes it difficult to benefit from increased data." Removing it is precisely the spirit of the question — treating the drafter less like a bolted-on feature-mimic and more like a free <b>token-predicting student</b> whose only job is to match the target's <i>output distribution</i>. The result: EAGLE-3 keeps scaling with more training data where EAGLE plateaued.</p>
<div class="callout green">
  <div class=" co-t">Reframed</div>
  <p>"Train the drafter on-policy like a distilled student" = HASS's harmonized context + EAGLE-3's training-time test + dropping the feature-MSE shackle. The field converged on the user's exact intuition.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- 05 -->
<section id="s-divergence" data-code="divergence">
<div class="section-no">05 — Which distillation loss?</div>
<h2>On-policy needs a divergence, and the choice matters</h2>
<p>Once you train on self-generated drafts, you still have to pick what the loss <i>compares</i>. Plain next-token cross-entropy throws away the target's full distribution; a distillation divergence keeps it. DistillSpec, OSD, and GKD all confirm that matching the target's <b>distribution</b> (not just its argmax) lifts acceptance — but the right divergence is <b>decoding-dependent</b>.</p>
<p>The two poles: <b>forward KL</b> is <i>mode-covering</i> (spreads mass to cover every token the target likes, plus some it doesn't), while <b>reverse KL</b> is <i>mode-seeking</i> (concentrates on the target's peak). For a peaky target under greedy/low-temperature decoding, the sharper reverse-KL drafter drafts the likely token more often and gets accepted more. Toggle them against the same target.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">divergence.viz — next-token distribution after "...capital of"</span><span class="vp-hint">toggle the loss</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="viz-controls">
      <button class="viz-btn active" id="div-rkl">Reverse KL (mode-seeking)</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="div-fkl">Forward KL (mode-covering)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="div-canvas" height="220"></canvas>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-num" id="div-acc">87%</div><div class="stat-cap">P(token accepted) = 1 − TV</div></div>
      <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-num" id="div-mode" style="color:var(--cyan)">sharp</div><div class="stat-cap">drafter shape</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="viz-caption" id="div-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The honest takeaway from DistillSpec is that no single divergence wins everywhere: forward-KL/JSD helped on summarization, reverse-KL with sampled data helped on GSM8K with temperature, and direct TVD optimization (despite being the quantity tied to acceptance) wasn't reliably best. The robust finding is the <b>data</b>, not the divergence: <i>on-policy, self-generated</i> data beat fixed ground-truth data across the board — and draft-generated data was nearly as good as target-generated data while costing far less to produce.</p>
</section>

