<!-- 07 -->
<section id="s-verdict" data-code="verdict">
<div class="section-no">07 — Verdict & nuance</div>
<h2>Yes — but "on-policy" ≠ "standalone"</h2>
<p>So is training the drafter on-policy, like a distilled student, a good idea? <b>Strongly yes.</b> It's the single highest-leverage change in modern drafter training, and the evidence (HASS, EAGLE-3, DistillSpec, OSD) is consistent. The one place to be careful is the word "<b>standalone</b>."</p>
<p>The user's framing bundles two separable ideas. Pull them apart:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>On-policy distillation</b> (train on the drafter's own drafts, match the target's distribution) — <b>clearly helps</b>. Adopt it.</li>
  <li><b>Decoupling</b> the drafter into a truly standalone LM that no longer reads the target's hidden states — <b>usually hurts</b>. Those rich, free features are exactly why a tiny EAGLE head reaches acceptance lengths a same-size standalone model can't touch. OSD's standalone drafters need <i>continuous online</i> distillation just to stay competitive.</li>
</ul>
<p>The sweet spot is what EAGLE-3 already lands on: keep the drafter's input "at least partially from the main model" (the user's own phrasing — the fused hidden states), but <b>train it like a student</b> — on-policy, distribution-matching, and freed from the feature-MSE leash. That's standalone in <i>objective</i>, attached in <i>input</i>.</p>

<div class="callout amber">
  <div class=" co-t">The catch — cost</div>
  <p>On-policy isn't free at <i>training</i> time: each step needs the drafter to generate, so unrolled training is several × slower than dumping a static tape. This is why SpecForge invests in decoupled target/draft execution and fused kernels, and why "Fast on-policy distillation" methods exist (2–47× via prefix truncation). The acceptance win is large enough to pay for it — but budget for it.</p>
</div>
<div class="callout">
  <div class=" co-t">Practical recipe</div>
  <p>(1) Warm-start the EAGLE/EAGLE-3 head teacher-forced for stability. (2) Switch to a 3–4 step training-time-test unroll so the drafter conditions on its own features. (3) Use a distribution-matching KD loss (top-K / reverse-KL is a strong default for greedy serving; pick per decoding strategy). (4) Drop the feature-regression loss. (5) If you serve a narrow, drifting query mix, keep distilling online (OSD).</p>
</div>
<p>In short: the instinct is right and the field already validated it. The drafter <i>should</i> be trained on-policy like a distilled student — just keep feeding it the target's features, because in speculative decoding that coupling is a feature, not a bug.</p>
</section>

